Spectral radiance $L$ for a blackbody at wavelength $\lambda=9\,\mu\text{m}$ is calculated via Planck's law: $L_\lambda = \frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5} \frac{1}{e^{hc/(\lambda k_B T)}-1}$. Substituting constants and $T=300\,\text{K}$ yields $1.15 \times 10^{-13}\,\text{W}/(\text{sr}\cdot\text{m}^2\cdot\mu\text{m})$; for $T=400\,\text{K}$, it yields $2.57 \times 10^{-13}\,\text{W}/(\text{sr}\cdot\text{m}^2\cdot\mu\text{m})$. The difference is $1.42 \times 10^{-13}\,\text{W}/(\text{sr}\cdot\text{m}^2\cdot\mu\text{m})$. Note that $1\,\text{m}^{-1} = 10^{-6}\,\mu\text{m}^{-1}$, so spectral density per meter is $10^6$ times smaller than per micrometer. A temperature difference ($\Delta T$) is not an optical-power calibration because radiance depends non-linearly on $T$ (Planck function); $\Delta L \neq f(\Delta T)$ linearly. Calibration requires mapping specific radiance values to power, not just temperature deltas.

